  "statement": "The accelerationist claim that pre-deployment safety gateways create a fatal innovation bottleneck mistakes routine software patches for high-stakes diagnostic determinations. When a hospital deploys unverified clinical intelligence that allocates critical care based on flawed billing codes rather than physiological need, the resulting triage errors become invisible to post-market telemetry. This occurs because the biased outputs contaminate the very electronic health record databases that subsequent validation cohorts rely upon to measure accuracy. Requiring deployment (safety-gated) protocols prevents these silent feedback loops from locking structural discrimination into permanent medical records before any independent auditor can inspect them.\n\nWhile critics argue that multi-year regulatory reviews ignore weekly iteration cycles, treating diagnostic intelligence like consumer applications ignores the irreversibility of physiological harm. Clinical systems operate under conditions where an uncorrected classification error results in permanent organ damage or death, making accountability (institutional) structures non-negotiable before market entry. The demand for upfront governance (oversight) is not a bureaucratic preference for delay, but a recognition that once a toxic algorithm is embedded in clinical workflows, no amount of retrospective auditing can restore the health of patients already misallocated. This institutional discipline ensures that human oversight remains an active barrier rather than a nominal stamp of approval.\n\nI note that no one has responded to my argument that unverified diagnostic intelligence introduces systematic allocation errors that escape detection. Skeptic proposes runtime audits as a lighter alternative to pre-market gates, yet runtime monitoring cannot catch failures generated by closed-loop data contamination. If the telemetry data itself is poisoned by prior algorithmic misallocations, any automated compliance monitoring tool looking at that data will verify the bias rather than expose it. Structural verification at the deployment threshold remains the only mechanism capable of intercepting unvalidated models before they compromise the evidentiary baseline of patient care.",
